25, -0.019500732421875, 0.005054473876953125, -0.0225982666015625, -0.0006399154663085938, 0.042449951171875, 0.030548095703125, 0.019866943359375, 0.0173797607421875, 0.01509857177734375, 0.0177459716796875, 0.0050506591796875, -0.038055419921875, -0.004970550537109375, 0.012664794921875, 0.0311737060546875, -0.00244140625, -0.00567626953125, 0.002338409423828125, -0.010711669921875, -0.037841796875, 0.0102691650390625, 0.0094757080078125, 0.01116180419921875, -0.0009679794311523438, -0.0248870849609375, -0.0169525146484375, -0.01080322265625, 0.00336456298828125, -0.017242431640625, 0.0218505859375, -0.0209503173828125, 0.032562255859375, 0.0204010009765625, -0.0220947265625, -0.0068359375, -0.005153656005859375, 0.025970458984375, -0.005702972412109375, 0.0290679931640625, 0.006748199462890625, -0.0284576416015625, 0.00064849853515625, -0.0136871337890625, 0.01337432861328125, 0.02813720703125, 0.002468109130859375, 0.00241851806640625, 0.020050048828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA)\u2014often referred to as Clifford algebra in the mathematical literature\u2014to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits [1,2]. While both terms describe algebras generated from a vector space with a quadratic form, \"Geometric Algebra\" emphasizes the geometric interpretation and use of multivectors (combinations of scalars, vectors, bivectors, etc.) unified by the geometric product, whereas \"Clifford algebra\" is typically used in a more abstract algebraic context [1,2]. The geometric product is associative and generally non-commutative, combining inner and outer products, and introduces a graded structure (decomposition into components of different grades). This non-commutativity and grading complicate direct extensions of classical Laplace transform definitions, which usually assume commutative multiplication and scalar-valued functions.\n\nExtensions of the Laplace transform to hyper-complex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras, have been studied [3,4]. These approaches often depend on properties like commutativity or the existence of a unique scalar exponential, which do not generally hold in GA due to the geometric product and the presence of multivectors of different grades. In GA, the exponential of a general multivector\u2014defined via its power series\u2014may not reduce to a scalar or vector, and the order of multiplication is significant. In our approach, we avoid the need for multivector exponentials by restricting the Laplace variable \\( s \\) to be a real or complex scalar, so that \\( e^{-st} \\) is always a scalar exponential. This ensures that multiplication by \\( e^{-st} \\) with each grade component is unambiguous and always performed from the left. This is in contrast to previous methods that may require more general exponentials or rely on commutativity.\n\nTo address the challenges posed by the graded, non-commutative structure of GA, we introduce a definition of the Laplace transform tailored to multivector-valued functions. Specifically, we define the Laplace transform by applying the classical Laplace transform separately to each grade component of the multivector-valued function. For a function \\( f(t) = \\sum_{k=0}^n f^{(k)}(t) \\) with grade-\\( k \\) components \\( f^{(k)}(t) \\), we define its Laplace transform as\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty \\left( \\sum_{k=0}^n e^{-st} f^{(k)}(t) \\right) dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real or complex scalar and \\( e^{-st} \\) multiplies each grade component from the left. This \"grade-wise\" application preserves the multivector structure and avoids ambiguities from non-commutativity. The linearity of the Laplace transform is preserved, as the transform acts linearly on each grade component. Invertibility is also maintained grade-wise, provided the classical Laplace transform is invertible for each component. For further discussion of these properties in the context of non-commutative algebras, see [4].\n\nWe focus on geometric algebras with signature less than or equal to 5, where the signature refers to the number of positive and negative squares in the quadratic form (e.g., (3,0) for three-dimensional Euclidean space). This restriction is motivated by the rapid growth in algebraic complexity: the dimension of the algebra doubles with each additional basis vector (\\( 2^n \\)), so signature 5 yields 32 basis elements, while signature 6 yields 64, significantly increasing computational and memory requirements. Symbolic multiplication tables and storage become prohibitive for higher signatures, and the time complexity of basic operations increases accordingly. Importantly, many physical systems of interest\u2014including those in robotics, electromagnetism, and classical mechanics\u2014can be effectively modeled within these lower signatures [2,5,6], so this restriction does not exclude most standard applications. However, certain high-dimensional systems (e.g., in quantum field theory) may fall outside this range.\n\nThe main contribution of this paper is the proposed definition of the Laplace transform in GA, along with a discussion of its key properties\u2014such as linearity and invertibility\u2014and its applicability to the analysis of dynamical systems. Compared to prior approaches for hyper-complex algebras [3,4], our method:\n- Explicitly addresses the challenges posed by the graded, non-commutative structure of GA.\n- Applies the Laplace transform independently to each grade component, preserving multivector structure.\n- Fixes the order of multiplication to avoid ambiguities from non-commutativity.\n- Enables the analysis of a broader class of multivector-valued systems than methods restricted to commutative or scalar-valued algebras.\n\nA summary of the grade-wise Laplace transform process is provided in Table 1, illustrating how each grade component is transformed independently and recombined to yield the multivector-valued Laplace transform.\n\nWe illustrate the use of our definition with original case studies drawn from electrical circuit analysis and rigid body dynamics modeled within GA. In these examples, the GA-based Laplace transform provides a unified framework for handling multivector-valued signals and operators, revealing geometric relationships and symmetries that may be obscured in traditional scalar or vector approaches.\n\nWhile our approach provides a systematic framework for Laplace analysis in GA, some limitations remain. The treatment of algebras with higher signatures or the development of efficient computational algorithms for large-scale systems are open questions for future work. Existing computational tools such as Gaalop and Clifford [5] may offer partial solutions for symbolic and numerical computations in higher-dimensional algebras, but further advances are needed to address scalability. Additionally, our method may not be directly applicable to dynamical systems involving infinite-dimensional algebras or to systems with non-polynomial nonlinearities that do not decompose grade-wise (e.g., nonlinearities involving products or functions of mixed-grade multivectors that cannot be separated by grade). Partial solutions may exist for certain classes of nonlinearities (such as those expressible as sums of grade-homogeneous terms), but a general theory remains to be developed. Problems where the physical interpretation of multivector exponentials is ambiguous, even within the chosen signature range, also present challenges.\n\nIn summary, our work establishes a foundation for Laplace analysis in Geometric Algebra by addressing key challenges posed by its graded and non-commutative structure, and demonstrating its utility through concrete examples. These contributions provide a basis for further development and motivate the structure of the remainder of the paper. In Section 2, we review relevant background on Geometric Algebra, clarify terminology, and summarize related work on Laplace transforms in hyper-complex and non-commutative algebras. Section 3 presents our definition of the Laplace transform in GA and discusses its mathematical properties. Section 4 demonstrates its application to representative dynamical systems. Finally, Section 5 discusses potential implications for the analysis and control of complex systems in engineering and physics, as well as open questions and directions for further research.\n\nReferences:\n[1] D. Hestenes and G. Sobczyk, \"Clifford Algebra to Geometric Calculus,\" Reidel, 1984.\n[2] C. Doran and A. Lasenby, \"Geometric Algebra for Physicists,\" Cambridge University Press, 2003.\n[3] S. Sangwine and T. Ell, \"Hypercomplex Fourier Transforms of Color Images,\" IEEE Transactions on Image Processing, vol. 16, no. 1, pp. 22-35, 2007.\n[4] E. Hitzer, \"Quaternion and Clifford Fourier Transforms,\" in Advances in Applied Clifford Algebras, vol. 17, no. 3, pp. 497-517, 2007.\n[5] A. De Keninck, T. B. Bouma, and L. Dorst, \"Geometric Algebra: A Computational Framework for Geometric Applications,\" Computers & Graphics, vol. 92, pp. 1-13, 2020.\n[6] J. M. Selig, \"Geometric Fundamentals of Robotics,\" 2nd ed., Springer, 2010.\n\nTable 1: Grade-wise Laplace Transform Process in Geometric Algebra\n\n| Grade \\( k \\) | Component \\( f^{(k)}(t) \\) | Laplace Transform \\( \\mathcal{L}[f^{(k)}](s) \\) |\n|--------------|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| 0 (scalar)   | \\( f^{(0)}(t) \\)           | \\( \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f^{(0)}(t) dt \\)    |\n| 1 (vector)   | \\( f^{(1)}(t) \\)           | \\( \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f^{(1)}(t) dt \\)    |\n| 2 (bivector) | \\( f^{(2)}(t) \\)           | \\( \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f^{(2)}(t) dt \\)    |\n| ...          | ...                        | ...                                           |\n| n            | \\( f^{(n)}(t) \\)           | \\( \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f^{(n)}(t) dt \\)    |\n\nThe total Laplace transform is \\( \\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\mathcal{L}[f^{(k)}](s) \\), preserving the multivector structure."}}